      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Pain is an almost universal experience, though the human pain sensory system can sometimes go awry, allowing pain to continue past its useful purpose. Managing pain is an imprecise science-almost an art. Approaching pain by type and learning how patients might communicate each different ache, throb, or twinge can improve treatment planning. Visceral/soft tissue pain, bone pain, neuropathic pain, pleuritic pain, colicky pain, or muscular pain manifest differently and respond to interventions differently. In the long-term care setting, cognitive impairment can be a barrier to assessing pain. For patients who have chronic pain, finding a drug regimen that works usually takes time and trials."},
